  "worked_solution": "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
